paragraph:
Welcome to math class! This chapter will introduce you to the mathematical practices that you will be using throughout this course and beyond. You will start by working with your classmates to make sense of and solve a series of challenging problems. These problems preview some of the mathematics that you will be learning throughout the year.

In Section 1.1, you will work on several challenging investigations with your team. You will revisit some of these later in the course. The main purpose of these investigations is to introduce some of the major conceptual ideas of this math course, such as Connections Across Representations, Number Relationships, and Knowns and Unknowns.

Then, in Section 1.2, you will develop ways to represent Number Relationships and make Connections Across Representations. You will represent your ideas using numbers, symbols, diagrams, words, and various kinds of tables. You will explore Knowns and Unknowns by writing equivalent expressions to represent situations.

In this chapter you will:
• analyze and recognize patterns;
• organize and represent data visually and interpret the data;
• use rough draft thinking and persevere in solving problems;
• use multiple representations to evaluate and compare expressions; and
• use arrays to determine the value of expressions.

This chapter begins with a focus on Number Relationships and Connections Across Representations as you explore multiple ways to represent portions, such as percents, decimals, and fractions. You will use 100% blocks as a tool to explore the relationships between the equivalent representations of portions.

In Section 3.2, you will also investigate Shapes and Distances as you describe how a point moves on a number line and locate the positions of integers. You will also learn to graph integer points in a coordinate plane, use absolute value, and determine the length of a line segment on a coordinate graph.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• use a powerful new tool for writing equivalent fractions;
• use percents, decimals, and fractions to describe a portion of whole;
• represent portions as percents, decimals, and fractions with pictures, symbols, and words;
• write the decimal form of a number when it is given as a percent or fraction;
• locate positive and negative integers and rational numbers on a number line;
• determine the absolute value of a number; and
• determine the length of horizontal and vertical line segments on a coordinate graph.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• divide whole and mixed numbers by fractions;
• make sense of division using a variety of methods, including long division;
• use the Order of Operations to determine the correct value of a numerical expression;
• use a variable to represent any number;
• write and evaluate variable expressions that represent area and perimeter; and
• combine like terms to create equivalent algebraic expressions.

Chapter 8 Statistics and Multiplication Equations

In Chapter 8, you will transition to the subject of statistics. In Section 8.1, you will build on your knowledge of Data Summaries to investigate statistical questions. Do you remember learning about the famous Calaveras County frog-jumping contest in Chapter 1? In Section 8.1, you will return to investigating Math in the World through the data from the frog-jumping contest. This time, you will make Connections Across Representations by using new tools to analyze and compare groups of frogs. You will also look at more ways to display and interpret the data.

In Section 8.2, you will work with Math in the World, revisiting math ideas you have already studied and learning new strategies to apply to new contexts. When traveling in a car, for example, have you ever asked, "When are we going to get there?" Did you know that you could use mathematics to answer that question? You will also use diagrams like the ones you used for percents to determine the relationship between distance, rate, and time in order to make Connections Across Representations. You will learn how to write and solve equations with variables to answer questions about Knowns and Unknowns. You will also learn how to compare quantities that are very different.

In this chapter, you will learn how to:
• decide if a question is a statistical question;
• use measures of central tendency, histograms, and box plots to represent and compare data;
• consider the shape and spread of data both through visual displays and calculations;
• solve problems involving distance, rate, and time; and
• convert units so that they are the same and then use them to compare rates.
